---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeCycleCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/UnitsLifeCycleCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ovulation-calculator">
  <UnitsLifeCycleCalculator client:load mode="ovulation" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Count backwards, not forwards</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful fact about ovulation timing: it depends on the next period, not the last one.
      The luteal phase (from ovulation to the start of bleeding) is very consistent for each person,
      usually 12 to 14 days. Almost all variation in cycle length is in the follicular phase before
      ovulation.
    </p>
    <p>
      So "day 14" is correct only for a 28-day cycle. On a 32-day cycle with a 14-day luteal phase,
      ovulation is about day 18. On a 24-day cycle it is about day 10. So this ovulation calculator asks
      for your luteal phase length as a separate input and does not assume half your cycle.
    </p>

    <h2>The six-day fertile window</h2>
    <p>
      Conception is possible on only about six days per cycle: the five days before ovulation and the
      day of ovulation. The asymmetry has a biological cause.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Cell</th><th scope="col">Survival</th><th scope="col">Consequence</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sperm</td><td>Up to 5 days in fertile cervical mucus</td><td>Intercourse before ovulation can still result in conception</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Egg</td><td>About 12&ndash;24 hours after release</td><td>The window closes almost immediately after ovulation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The probability is not equal across those six days. Studies of couples who time intercourse find
      the highest conception rates in the two days just before ovulation. The day of ovulation is
      slightly lower, and the fifth day before it is much lower.
    </p>

    <h2>What a calendar prediction can and cannot do</h2>
    <p>
      This tool projects from an average. It cannot know that you ovulated late this month because of
      illness, travel, stress or a change in sleep. A variation of several days between cycles is
      normal, also in people who describe their cycles as regular. Large data sets of tracked cycles
      show that only a minority of people ovulate on the day a calendar method predicts.
    </p>
    <p>Three methods observe and do not predict, and they work well with this tool:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        LH test strips detect the luteinising hormone surge 24&ndash;36 hours before ovulation. Start
        to test a few days before the predicted date.
      </li>
      <li>
        Basal body temperature rises by about 0.3 &deg;C after ovulation and stays up. It confirms
        ovulation, but only afterwards.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cervical mucus becomes clear, stretchy and slippery in the fertile window. It is the cheapest
        and most immediate signal, and fertility awareness teaches it first.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Not a contraceptive method</h2>
    <p>
      Calendar-only prediction is one of the least reliable ways to avoid pregnancy, with typical-use
      failure rates of 12% to 24% per year. Correctly taught symptothermal fertility awareness
      (temperature, mucus and calendar rules, with instruction) works much better, but it is a
      different practice from reading a date off a web page. Do not use this tool as contraception.
    </p>

    <h2>When to seek advice</h2>
    <p>
      General guidance in most health systems is to seek advice after twelve months of trying under
      the age of 35, or six months at 35 and over. Sooner if cycles are absent, very irregular,
      shorter than 21 days or longer than 35, or if there is a known condition such as PCOS or
      endometriosis. Also mention a short luteal phase (under 10 days).
    </p>

    <h2>Why this page has no account and no cookies</h2>
    <p>
      Reproductive health data is some of the most sensitive information a person can enter into a
      website. An app that uploads, stores and syncs it makes a permanent record that can be breached,
      sold or subpoenaed. Here the arithmetic runs in your browser, and no request leaves the page while
      you use it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Saving your dates on this device is off by default. If you switch it on, the values go to your own
      browser&apos;s local storage, and the delete button removes them at once. To confirm, open your
      browser&apos;s network tab, or turn off Wi-Fi: the tool works the same offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
